HOME_ROADMAP_18 = [
("ceiling-fan-direction-summer-winter", "understand",
"Which way should your ceiling fan spin? The seasonal switch",
"Counterclockwise in summer, clockwise on low in winter - the ENERGY STAR-backed setting that changes how your room feels, plus the rule everyone forgets.",
"""<p>Most ceiling fans have a small reverse switch nobody has ever touched. It is the difference between your fan helping and merely spinning - and the correct setting changes with the seasons.</p>
<h2>Summer: counterclockwise (downdraft)</h2>
<p>In hot months, run the fan counterclockwise when viewed from below - you should feel a direct breeze standing under it. The moving air accelerates moisture evaporating off your skin, so the room <i>feels</i> several degrees cooler than the thermostat reads, at the cost of a lightbulb's worth of power. That wind-chill effect is why <a href="/home/ceiling-fan-vs-standing-fan/">fans and air coolers are comfort devices, not thermometers</a> - and it pairs with, not replaces, the cooling strategy in <a href="/home/thermostat-settings-that-save-money/">the thermostat guide</a>.</p>
<h2>Winter: clockwise, on low (updraft)</h2>
<p>Warm air rises and pools at the ceiling while you sit in the cooler half of the room. Running the fan <b>clockwise on the lowest speed</b> draws air gently up and outwards, easing that warm layer down the walls without blowing a draft on anyone. You redistribute heat you have already paid for - a small, real contribution to heating comfort, which is why energy agencies recommend it rather than one of those appliance myths.</p>
<h2>The rule everyone forgets</h2>
<p>Fans cool people, not rooms - the breeze works on skin, not on air temperature. Run one all day in an empty room and you have paid to stir nothing. Off when you leave, every time; the energy agencies put that line in their guidance because it is where the actual savings live.</p>
<h2>The switch and the sanity check</h2>
<p>The reverse switch sits on the motor housing (or the remote). Stand under the fan on high: breeze on your skin means downdraft (summer) - no breeze, just upward flow, means updraft (winter). If the wobble appears when you flip it, that is a balance or mounting issue, not the direction: <a href="/home/ceiling-fan-mounting-right/">the mounting fix is here</a>, and the fan's own <a href="/home/standby-power-real-numbers-your-meter/">standing power draw is worth a meter check while you are at it</a>. Two switches a year, thirty seconds each - the cheapest comfort adjustment in the house.</p>"""),

("washing-machine-hoses-replace", "maintain",
"The hoses behind your washing machine: the cheap flood-prevention check",
"Rubber washer hoses age like tyres, and they burst with the house asleep. The inspection, the upgrade, and the ten-dollar valve habits that protect the floor below.",
"""<p>The two hoses feeding your washing machine are under constant water pressure, every hour of every day - and when one fails, it fails completely. A burst washer hose can release hundreds of litres an hour into a room nobody is standing in, which is why the little valves behind the machine deserve ten minutes of your year.</p>
<h2>The inspection (twice a year)</h2>
<p>Slide the machine out (unplug it first, and mind the drain hose), then run your fingers along both supply hoses looking for: bulges or ballooning, cracks - especially near the fittings - rust stains at the connections, kinks, and any brittleness. Turn the machine's valves off and on once while you are there; a valve that has not moved in years is often the second failure waiting. Anything soft, swollen or cracked gets replaced this week, not "when there's time" - <a href="/home/how-to-shut-off-water-main/">and make sure you can kill the whole supply if a hose lets go mid-cycle</a>.</p>
<h2>The upgrade that ends the worry</h2>
<p>Old-style rubber hoses are the failure case. Braided stainless-steel supply hoses are inexpensive, available at any hardware shop, and vastly more resistant to bursting - replacing rubber with braided steel is the single cheapest flood-prevention upgrade in the house. While manufacturers' and insurers' guidance commonly frames rubber hoses as worth replacing within a few years, the honest rule is simpler: <b>inspect often, upgrade to steel once, and stop thinking about it.</b></p>
<h2>The habits that do the rest</h2>
<p>Leave a hand's width between machine and wall so hoses don't kink. Shut both valves off if you travel for more than a few weeks - a small ritual that removes the entire risk while you are away. And know the fixture valves' location well enough to show someone else - <a href="/home/washing-machine-wont-drain/">the machine's other common failure, the no-drain, is a different and cheaper fix</a>.</p>
<p>If the worst has already happened, the sequence matters: valves off, water up with towels and a wet vacuum fast, and the room dried properly - <a href="/home/mould-after-a-flooded-room/">because damp that lingers becomes mould</a>, and <a href="/home/hidden-water-leak-meter-test/">the meter test catches the slow leaks this checklist can't see</a>. Ten minutes and the price of two hoses - versus the floor below yours.</p>"""),

("attic-insulation-basics", "maintain",
"Attic insulation: the upgrade that pays every single month",
"Heat leaves and enters through the top of the house first. The air-seal-first rule, depth by climate, and where the DIY line sits.",
"""<p>If your home has an under-insulated attic - and a startling share do - you are paying to heat the sky. Insulation is the rare upgrade that pays a little every single day, in every season: less heat escaping in winter, less heat pouring down in summer.</p>
<h2>Why the attic, specifically</h2>
<p>Warm air rises, so the top of the building is where conditioned air pushes hardest to escape - and in summer, a sun-cooked roof radiates down into the rooms below. Topping up loft or attic insulation attacks both directions at once, and it usually costs a fraction of what the same money would buy in new equipment.</p>
<h2>Step one is not insulation - it is air sealing</h2>
<p>Insulation slows heat flow; it does not stop warm air leaking through gaps around light fittings, hatches, pipes and wiring holes. Sealing those penetrations first is the rule the energy agencies lead with, and skipping it wastes a chunk of the insulation's potential - <a href="/home/ice-dam-prevention-checklist/">the ice-dam guide covers the same seal-first logic for snow country</a>, where it does double duty.</p>
<h2>How much is enough</h2>
<p>Insulation is rated in R-value - resistance to heat flow - and the recommended depth depends on where you live: colder climates call for substantially deeper levels than mild ones. rather than quoting a single number, the honest move is checking your national energy agency's zone map (the US Department of Energy publishes one; the UK's Energy Saving Trust does the equivalent) and measuring what your attic currently has - many homes have a fraction of the recommended depth. If you can see the tops of the joists, there is room to work.</p>
<h2>DIY or professional</h2>
<p>Rolls and batts laid perpendicular across the joists are a realistic weekend DIY for open, walkable attics - masks, gloves and long sleeves non-negotiable with glass-fibre. Blown-in insulation fills irregular spaces better and needs a blowing machine - often loaned with bulk purchase - and it is where a professional crew earns its fee, along with anything involving recessed lights, restricted crawl spaces, or wiring questions. Keep soffit vents clear as you go - <a href="/home/condensation-ventilation-that-works/">insulation that blocks ventilation creates the damp problems it was meant to prevent</a>.</p>
<p>Pair the project with the cheap wins around it: <a href="/home/thermostat-settings-that-save-money/">the setback schedule</a>, <a href="/home/hvac-filter-sizes-and-merv/">a fresh filter</a>, and <a href="/home/standby-power-real-numbers-your-meter/">the standby-load audit</a> - insulation is the biggest lever, and these are the rest of the toolbox.</p>"""),

("frozen-pipe-prevention", "maintain",
"Frozen pipes: the winter checklist that prevents the burst",
"Water expands as it freezes, and pipes split quietly. The preparation list for the first hard freeze, the drip trick, and the first five minutes if one lets go.",
"""<p>A frozen pipe is a delayed flood. The ice itself does not usually burst the pipe at the freeze point - it blocks the line, and the pressure between the blockage and the closed tap splits it somewhere else. Which is why prevention, done before the first hard freeze, is the entire game.</p>
<h2>Before winter: the prep list</h2>
<p><b>Insulate the vulnerable runs.</b> Pipes in unheated spaces - crawlspaces, garages, exterior walls, that one freezing utility cupboard - are the candidates. Foam pipe sleeves are cheap and cut with scissors.</p>
<p><b>Seal the drafts.</b> Cold air moving over a pipe freezes it faster than cold air sitting still - the same physics the attic checklist uses: <a href="/home/ice-dam-prevention-checklist/">air-sealing pays everywhere</a>.</p>
<p><b>Drain and disconnect outdoors.</b> Garden taps and their hoses: hose off, tap drained if it has an internal shut-off. Water left in an exterior line is the classic split.</p>
<p><b>Know the main shutoff cold.</b> If a pipe ever bursts, the first sixty seconds are the shut-off valve and nothing else - <a href="/home/how-to-shut-off-water-main/">locate it and test it today, not in an emergency</a>.</p>
<h2>During a hard freeze</h2>
<p>Let the vulnerable taps drip slowly - moving water is far harder to freeze, and a running tap costs pennies against a plumber's emergency call. Open the cupboard doors under sinks on outside walls so room warmth reaches the pipes. Keep the home heated even when away - a thermostat set lower, never off, in cold snaps: <a href="/home/thermostat-settings-that-save-money/">the setback guidance caps out before "off" does</a>.</p>
<h2>If a pipe freezes - or bursts</h2>
<p>Frozen but intact: shut-off off at the main for safety, then thaw gently along the pipe - warm (not boiling) cloths, a hairdryer on low, working from the tap backwards. Never an open flame or a blast furnace heat gun; steam inside a pipe is its own explosion. Burst: main off, taps open to drain pressure, towels and buckets, then the repair call - and dry the space fast, <a href="/home/mould-after-a-flooded-room/">because the clock on mould starts immediately</a>. Any water near electrics means the electrics win: power off first, and <a href="/home/electrical-fire-warning-signs/">treat any after-damp socket with suspicion</a>. <a href="/home/autumn-home-preparation/">The autumn checklist slots this in before the first freeze each year</a> - the cheapest insurance policy in the house.</p>"""),

("mice-in-the-house-signs", "guide",
"Signs of mice in the house - and the first-weekend response",
"Droppings, gnaw marks, midnight ceiling scritters. How to confirm, how to close the door behind them, and the honest line where a professional takes over.",
"""<p>Autumn sends mice house-hunting, and they need a hole the width of a biro to move in. Confirming the infestation early and responding in the right order is the difference between a weekend errand and a winter flatmate.</p>
<h2>The signs, most reliable first</h2>
<p><b>Droppings</b> - small, dark, rice-grain pellets along walls, behind appliances, in cupboards. Fresh ones are soft and dark. <b>Gnaw marks</b> - on food packaging, skirting boards, cables (gnawed wiring is a genuine fire risk: <a href="/home/electrical-fire-warning-signs/">which makes this a safety issue, not just a nuisance</a>). <b>Sounds</b> - scratching or scurrying at night, from ceilings, walls or under floors. <b>Smears</b> - dark grease marks along regular run routes. <b>Nesting material</b> - shredded paper or fabric in quiet corners. One sign is suspicion; a cluster is confirmation.</p>
<h2>The response, in the right order</h2>
<p><b>1. Seal first, trap second.</b> New mice replace trapped mice indefinitely if the door stays open. Sweep the exterior wall line: gaps around pipes, vents and door bottoms get packed with steel wool and sealed over - mice cannot chew through steel wool easily, and it holds caulk in place.</p>
<p><b>2. Starve them.</b> Food in glass or metal containers, floors crumbed, pet food not left out overnight, bins with lids. A sealed house with no calories is a bad tenancy.</p>
<p><b>3. Trap along the run routes.</b> Snap traps perpendicular to walls, trigger side against the skirting, where you found droppings - baited with peanut butter, checked daily. This is standard homeowner territory; humane live traps work too, but release must be far away or the mouse commutes home.</p>
<h2>Where the professional line sits</h2>
<p>Droppings in multiple rooms, signs that persist after two honest weeks of sealing and trapping, noises you cannot localise, or anything involving a business or rental property: bring in pest control. That is not defeat - populations behind walls do not respond to enthusiasm - <a href="/home/diy-vs-professional-pests/">the honest DIY-or-pro sorting is here</a>.</p>
<p>Prevention next autumn is the same list done in September: walk the exterior, seal the gaps, mind the food - <a href="/home/ants-in-the-kitchen/">the ants guide covers the kitchen half of the same discipline</a>, and <a href="/home/ignore-single-pest-sighting/">one sighting handled calmly is the rule for everything that crawls</a>.</p>"""),

("window-film-for-heat", "understand",
"Window film for heat: does it actually work?",
"Solar-control film can take real load off sun-baked rooms - if the type, glass and expectations all line up. The honest sorting, including the night-time trade-off.",
"""<p>On a summer afternoon, sun-facing glass is a heater running in reverse - and window film promises to fix it for a fraction of new glazing. Some of that promise is real. Here is what the film actually does, and where it disappoints.</p>
<h2>What the film does</h2>
<p>Solar-control films are thin layers applied to existing glass that reflect or absorb a meaningful share of the sun's energy before it enters the room. The effect people notice first is on hot-afternoon rooms - west and south-facing glass especially: less blazing glare, less radiant heat on skin near the window, furniture that stops fading. It is a legitimate tool in hot climates and on problem elevations - the same load-shedding logic as <a href="/home/solar-panels-worth-it-2026/">thinking about what your roof and windows actually receive</a>, only cheaper by far.</p>
<h2>The honest limits</h2>
<p><b>It is not insulation.</b> Film cuts solar gain; it does not meaningfully insulate against cold - winter heating bills barely notice it. <b>Results vary by glass and film.</b> The percentages manufacturers advertise assume ideal conditions; performance depends on film type (reflective, ceramic, low-e coatings), the glass it sits on, and which direction the window faces. Ask for the film's spec sheet and treat one-size-fits-all claims sceptically. <b>Some windows dislike film.</b> Certain coated or double-glazed units can trap heat and stress the glass - a professional installer checks compatibility, and that check is worth the call.</p>
<h2>The trade-off nobody mentions at purchase</h2>
<p>Reflective film turns your windows into mirrors from outside during the day - and from <i>inside</i> after dark, when your lit rooms become the visible ones. On ground floors facing streets, that is a genuine privacy reversal; blinds or curtains still earn their place. Appearance rules and approvals also apply in some buildings - worth knowing before the ladder comes out.</p>
<h2>DIY or pro</h2>
<p>Flat, small windows are a realistic careful-DIY job with a squeegee and patience; large panes bubble and trap dust without experience. Professional fitting costs more and looks invisible - on picture windows, that is usually the right call.</p>
<p>Film works best as one layer of the summer stack: <a href="/home/thermostat-settings-that-save-money/">setback settings</a>, <a href="/home/ceiling-fan-direction-summer-winter/">fans moving air on the right direction</a>, and shading outside beating any film at all. Glass is the problem child; the sun is the problem - film just argues with it.</p>"""),
]
